\u2019t help much.  \n- **B7**: only 2 left, and it\u2019s already in my meld, so redundant in hand.  \n- **W7**: 4 left, safe.  \n\nBetter to discard a suited tile that is not a terminal, to keep terminals/honors for possible Outside Hand or Pung of Terminals/Honors.  \n\n**W6** is a simple tile (6), not terminal, not honor, and I have no other W6. Discarding it reduces simplicity but keeps terminals/honors.  \n\nBut also consider: if I discard B7, I lose a terminal? B7 is not terminal (terminal is 1 or 9). So B7 is safe to discard and might be less useful.  \n\nActually, B7 is in the meld, so having an extra B7 in hand is useless unless I get two more for a pung, but only 2 left in wall. So B7 is a good discard.  \n\nAlso, F3 is an honor, could be useful for Pung of Honors (1 point each), but I only have one. Still, keeping honors helps for All Types or Outside Hand.  \n\nSo between B7 and W6:  \n- B7: only 2 left, but already used in meld, so less chance to form another meld with it.  \n- W6: more left, but might form chow with W5/W7? I have W7, but no W5. Unlikely.  \n\nI think discard **B7** is reasonable.  \n\nFinal choice: **Play B7**.  \n\nReason: B7 is redundant since it\u2019s already part of an exposed chow; discarding it preserves terminals and honors for potential high-scoring patterns like Outside Hand or Pung of Terminals/Honors, and it\u2019s relatively safe given only 2 left in the wall but not highly dangerous based on discard history.  \n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
