is a complete hand waiting for W3. But currently, without W3, I have leftover W1, W2, W8. That could also become a pair of W8 if I draw another W8, and then W1 and W2 become a Chow if I draw W3? Actually, if I draw W8, then I have W8 pair, and W1 W2 remain, which need W3 to become Chow, but then I would have two pairs (F4 and W8) which is not allowed; winning hand needs exactly one pair. So better to keep the F4 pair and aim for Chow with W1-W2-W3. So the plan: keep Chow W7-W8-W9, keep pair F4, and try to complete Chow W1-W2-W3. That means I need W3. So my hand is waiting for W3 to complete the Chow. But currently, I have an extra tile because I need to discard one. After forming Chow W7-W8-W9 (using one W7, one W8, one W9), I have left: W1, W2, W8, F4, F4. That's 5 tiles. But I need to discard one to have 4 hand tiles? Wait, total hand tiles: 8. If I mentally set aside Chow W7-W8-W9 (3 tiles), I have 5 tiles left: W1, W2, W8, F4, F4. I need to discard one to have 4 hand tiles? Actually, after discard, I will have 7 hand tiles total. If I consider Chow W7-W8-W9 as part of hand (not melded), then after discard, I have 7 hand tiles including that Chow. So from the 8 current hand tiles, I discard one, leaving 7. Among those 7, I can have Chow W7-W8-W9 (3 tiles), and the remaining 4 tiles: W1, W2, F4, F4. That's 4 tiles: W1, W2, and pair F4. That is not a complete hand because I need one more meld from W1 and W2? Actually, with 7 tiles: Chow (3), pair (2), and two singles W1 and W2. That's 3+2+2=7. So I need one more tile to make either a Chow with W1-W2 (need W3) or a pung with F4 (need another F4) or another pair, etc. So after discard, I will be one away from tenpai. So I need to choose a discard that leaves the best shape. If I discard W8 (the extra W8), then my hand becomes: Chow W7-W8-W9, pair F4, singles W1, W2. That is a good shape: I am waiting for W3 to complete Chow W1-W2-W3, which would give me two chows in hand plus two exposed chows and pair F4. That is te