thers can pung. W8 has 2 left, slightly more risky. W1 has 4 left, safe. But discarding W1 loses your pair candidate. Best is to discard W9, keep W1 pair and W2 for possible chow/pung, and W8 for possible chow if draw W7. However, W7-W8-W9 meld already exists, so extra W8 not useful for chow unless new sequence W6-W7-W8? You have W4-W5-W6 meld, so W6 is used. Actually, you could form a new chow with W8 if you draw W6-W7-W8 or W7-W8-W9, but W7-W8-W9 already melded, so duplicate. So W8 is less useful.  \n\nReason: I will discard W9 because it is a terminal with only one unseen, making it less likely to be useful for others to complete a pung, and it helps move toward All Simples if I can pair W2 or W8 (though W8 is terminal? W8 is simple, yes). Actually W8 is simple (6-8 are simple), W9 is terminal. Discarding W9 reduces terminals in hand. Keeping W1 pair would break All Simples, but I could change pair later if draw another W2. Immediate goal is to get closer to a winning hand: I need one meld and a pair. With two W1, I can use them as pair and try to pung W2, or use W2 as pair and pung W1. Discarding W9 keeps options open.  \n\nAnswer: Play W9"}
