in if I keep W1 as concealed triple and W2 waiting.  \n\nIf I Gang now, I must discard a tile after Gang (unless it\u2019s my turn?), but here valid actions are only Peng/Gang/Pass on the discarded W1. If I Gang, I replace a tile from the wall. Then my hand: W2 only. Need another W2 to win \u2014 possible with 4 left. But it\u2019s a big gamble.  \n\nBetter choice: **Peng W1** also risky, since I\u2019d have to discard W2 or another tile? But after Peng, I\u2019d have: exposed pung W1 + W2, still need a pair or meld for W2. So still incomplete.  \n\nActually, if I **Pass**, I keep W1,W1,W1,W2 \u2014 this is almost ready: I can win on a W2 draw (pair completes hand: 3 melds + pung W1 + pair W2). Also possible to win on W1 draw (then W1 becomes kong, hand: kong W1 + pair W2 + 3 chows).  \n\nBut if someone discards W1 now and I pass, they might discard another W1 later and I could gang then.  \n\nConsidering scoring: Concealed pung W1 gives \u201cPung of Terminals or Honors\u201d (2 pts) if W1 is terminal, yes. W1 is terminal. Keeping it concealed also gives chance for \u201cConcealed Hand\u201d if I win by self-draw later, or \u201cFully Concealed Hand\u201d if I never claim. Already I claimed chows earlier (B6 and W5, W8 from history) so not fully concealed.  \n\nThe best tactical move: **Pass**, because:  \n- Keeps flexibility: can still win by drawing W2 (pair), or drawing W1 (kong + pair W2).  \n- Avoids exposing W1 pung unnecessarily (exposed reduces patterns).  \n- Unshown W2 tiles are 4, reasonable chance.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nPassing keeps the hand maximally flexible and preserves concealed pung points. A gang now would leave me needing exactly W2 with only one tile in hand, reducing winning chances drastically. Peng would expose W1 and still leave me needing a pair without significantly improving readiness.  \n\n**Answer:** Pass"}
